# Line endings.
#
# Everything text is stored and checked out as LF, on every platform. The repository was
# already 488 files LF to 79 CRLF, with three files mixed inside themselves; that spread
# meant an edit made on Windows could rewrite a whole file's worth of line endings and bury
# a real change in thousands of lines of noise.
#
# eol=lf rather than plain `text=auto` deliberately: `text=auto` alone normalises the index
# but checks out the platform's native ending, so the working tree here would turn CRLF and
# no longer match what is committed. Pinning both keeps what you see equal to what git has.
# .NET, MSBuild and every editor in use read LF without complaint, and the two Windows-only
# script types in the tree (.ps1, .iss) were already LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never touched, never diffed as text. `text=auto` would infer this correctly, but
# an inference is a thing that can go wrong on a file that happens to look textual.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.snk binary
*.mkv binary
